":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":517.13399756,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":353.64000000000004,"r":357.76054687000004,"t":524.37085744},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/41","text":"v"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"i","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":516.34047368,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":357.72008000000005,"r":360.38010310000004,"t":521.7143181},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/42","text":"i"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"l","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":516.06487548,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":360.36031,"r":362.75134360000004,"t":519.5767060799999},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/43","text":"l"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"is incident to exactly one chord in","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":516.8630138199999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":366.72008000000005,"r":492.8621543425773,"t":524.4505585399999},"charspan":[0,35],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/44","text":"is incident to exactly one chord in"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"C","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":517.1339975599999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":495.60008000000005,"r":501.6334532700001,"t":524.3708574399999},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/45","text":"C"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"to it in C . Let v i l y be one such chord; then we show that y lies in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ) in the next claim.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":445.9189447999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.20038999999991,"r":532.663281696,"t":472.40639999999996},"charspan":[0,110],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/46","text":"to it in C . Let v i l y be one such chord; then we show that y lies in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ) in the next claim."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"Claim 6.6. y lies in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ) .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":423.74312381999994,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.20053199999995,"r":239.02737399999995,"t":437.48609999999996},"charspan":[0,42],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/47","text":"Claim 6.6. y lies in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ) ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Proof. If y does not lie in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ), then y must lie in the path between v i l and x ′ 1 (or the path between v i l and v ′ 1 ). If it lies on the path between v i l and x ′ 1 (resp. between v i l and v ′ 1 ), then we have a path from v i l to x ′ (resp. between v i l and v ′ ) which has length smaller than d ( v i l , x ′ ) (resp. d ( v i l , v ′ )), which is a contradiction. Hence, y lies in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ) (refer to Figure 12b).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":344.9986148,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19995800000004,"r":532.7834486369278,"t":414.806},"charspan":[0,451],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/48","text":"Proof. If y does not lie in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ), then y must lie in the path between v i l and x ′ 1 (or the path between v i l and v ′ 1 ). If it lies on the path between v i l and x ′ 1 (resp. between v i l and v ′ 1 ), then we have a path from v i l to x ′ (resp. between v i l and v ′ ) which has length smaller than d ( v i l , x ′ ) (resp. d ( v i l , v ′ )), which is a contradiction. Hence, y lies in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ) (refer to Figure 12b)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Note that d ( x ′ 1 , y ) ≥ d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ) and d ( v ′ 1 , y ) ≥ d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ). If not, then there exists a shorter or equal length path from v ′ to v i l (from x ′ to v i l ) that bypasses the edge v i l v (resp. v i l x ), which is a contradiction to the fact that the pair v i l , v ′ (resp. v i l , x ′ ) monitors the edge v i l v (resp. v i l x ). Hence d P ( x ′ 1 , v ′ 1 ) = d ( x ′ 1 , y ) + d ( y, v ′ 1 ) ≥ d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ) + d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ), implying d P ( x ′ 1 , v ′ 1 ) = d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ) + d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ). This implies that d ( x ′ 1 , y ) = d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ) and d ( v ′ 1 , y ) = d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ), which implies that the vertex y is unique in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ). This fact together with Claim 6.6 implies that in C , v i l is incident to exactly one chord.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":234.22093368000037,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.2,"r":532.8551564838398,"t":334.5494144},"charspan":[0,806],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/49","text":"Note that d ( x ′ 1 , y ) ≥ d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ) and d ( v ′ 1 , y ) ≥ d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ). If not, then there exists a shorter or equal length path from v ′ to v i l (from x ′ to v i l ) that bypasses the edge v i l v (resp. v i l x ), which is a contradiction to the fact that the pair v i l , v ′ (resp. v i l , x ′ ) monitors the edge v i l v (resp. v i l x ). Hence d P ( x ′ 1 , v ′ 1 ) = d ( x ′ 1 , y ) + d ( y, v ′ 1 ) ≥ d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ) + d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ), implying d P ( x ′ 1 , v ′ 1 ) = d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ) + d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ). This implies that d ( x ′ 1 , y ) = d ( x ′ 1 , v i l ) and d ( v ′ 1 , y ) = d ( v ′ 1 , v i l ), which implies that the vertex y is unique in P ( v ′ 1 , x ′ 1 ). This fact together with Claim 6.6 implies that in C , v i l is incident to exactly one chord."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Now note that if v and y are not adjacent, then the induced 2-path yv i l v is part of a chordless cycle of length at least 4, which is a contradiction. Hence vy ∈ E ( G k +1 ). Similarly, it can be shown that xy ∈ E ( G k +1 ). Hence vv i l x is part of a 4-cycle vv i l xyv , which leads to a contradiction. Hence v ′ and x ′ monitor vv i l .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":173.74083368000015,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.20024999999981,"r":532.9205391999997,"t":230.48640000000034},"charspan":[0,344],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/50","text":"Now note that if v and y are not adjacent, then the induced 2-path yv i l v is part of a chordless cycle of length at least 4, which is a contradiction. Hence vy ∈ E ( G k +1 ). Similarly, it can be shown that xy ∈ E ( G k +1 ). Hence vv i l x is part of a 4-cycle vv i l xyv , which leads to a contradiction. Hence v ′ and x ′ monitor vv i l ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Hence, until now, we showed that all edges in E l can be monitored by the vertices of Man ( G k ) \\ { v i l } . Hence, combining this fact with Claim 6.1 and 6.3, we can conclude that Man ( G k +1 ) forms an optimal MEG set of G k +1 . Hence, meg ( G ) = | Man ( G ) | for every strongly chordal graph G .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":114.71924480000018,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.20020999999969,"r":532.7840610681595,"t":170.00670000000014},"charspan":[0,305],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/51","text":"Hence, until now, we showed that all edges in E l can be monitored by the vertices of Man ( G k ) \\ { v i l } . Hence, combining this fact with Claim 6.1 and 6.3, we can conclude that Man ( G k +1 ) forms an optimal MEG set of G k +1 . Hence, meg ( G ) = | Man ( G ) | for every strongly chordal graph G ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"7. Conclusion and future aspects","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":83.59929514666669,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.2,"r":273.8567671849472,"t":94.76864},"charspan":[0,32],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/52","text":"7. Conclusion and future aspects"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In this paper, we solved the complexity status of the MIN-MEG problem for some well-known graph classes. Next, it will be interesting to address the following questions:","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":46.55944480000005,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19990000000001,"r":532.719728595968,"t":72.92660000000001},"charspan":[0,169],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/53","text":"In this paper, we solved the complexity status of the MIN-MEG problem for some well-known graph classes. Next, it will be interesting to address the following questions:"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"17","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":16.679344799999967,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":300.11990000000003,"r":311.84602127206404,"t":28.64650000000006},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/54","text":"17"}],"version":"1.10.0"}